Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.10.5.10", "inferred_role": "internet_exposed_rdp_gateway", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:34:48+0200", "last_seen": "2026-09-22T11:51:08+0200", "agent_name": "RDP-GW-01"},
    {"ip": "10.10.20.8", "inferred_role": "lateral_movement_target_internal_fileserver", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:49:08+0200", "last_seen": "2026-09-22T11:49:21+0200", "agent_name": "FILESVR-INT-02"},
    {"ip": "91.219.236.87", "inferred_role": "external_attacker_source", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:34:48+0200", "last_seen": "2026-09-22T11:51:08+0200"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Service Probe", "technique": "T1595.001", "what_happened": "At 2026-09-22 11:34:48 CEST, Suricata logged 'ET SCAN Behavioral Unusual Port 3389 traffic, Potential Scan or Infection' from external IP 91.219.236.87 to perimeter address mapping to RDP-GW-01 (10.10.5.10). The probe is a typical pre-attack RDP service-availability validation: the attacker TCP-connects to port 3389 to confirm the service is alive and that NLA / banner responses indicate a target worth attacking.", "chain_reason": "Suricata rule 'ET SCAN Behavioral Unusual Port 3389' on data.src_ip=91.219.236.87 → data.dest_ip=10.10.5.10. The 24-second gap before the brute-force burst begins is consistent with a typical scan-then-launch attacker workflow.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:34:48+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Credential Access (Brute Force)", "technique": "T1110.001 + T1021.001", "what_happened": "At 11:35:12 CEST, Event 4625 'An account failed to log on' fired in a high-volume burst on RDP-GW-01: SIEM-aggregated 2,847 failures in 58 seconds, ALL with logonType=10 (RemoteInteractive = RDP), ALL with ipAddress=91.219.236.87, ALL targeting the built-in 'administrator' account. The rate (~49 fails/sec) is ~600× the RDP-GW-01 baseline (~5 fails/min). After the high-volume burst at 11:36:10, the attacker throttled to ~1 failure every 1-3 minutes (continuation phase) — likely to avoid simple rate-based defenses. The built-in 'administrator' is EXEMPT from default account-lockout policy = the attack could continue indefinitely.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4625 burst correlation rule (rule.id 60221, level 12). Univariate signature: single external src ipAddress, single targetUserName (administrator), single logonType (10 = RDP), high count in short window.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:35:12+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Valid Accounts", "technique": "T1078 + T1021.001", "what_happened": "At 11:47:22 CEST (~12 minutes after the brute-force burst began), Event 4624 'An account was successfully logged on' fired on RDP-GW-01 with targetUserName=administrator, logonType=10 (RDP), ipAddress=91.219.236.87, workstationName=WIN-K8H2P4R3 (attacker-client default Windows-generated hostname, NOT corporate naming convention). The brute-force succeeded — the attacker now has interactive desktop access to the RDP gateway with full local-administrator rights.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 (rule.id 60224, level 13). The 4624 fires after the SAME source IP that produced the 2,847 4625 failures. Linkage: ipAddress + targetUserName + logonType + agent.name.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:47:22+0200"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Discovery / Account Enumeration", "technique": "T1033 + T1087.001 + T1087.002", "what_happened": "Between 11:47:58 and 11:48:31, the attacker executed three rapid recon commands under the freshly-authenticated CORP\\administrator session on RDP-GW-01: (a) 11:47:58 — whoami /priv (Windows-privilege enumeration: SeDebugPrivilege, SeImpersonatePrivilege, SeBackupPrivilege are the attacker-interesting ones — SeDebug enables LSASS access for credential dumping, SeImpersonate enables Print Spooler / RPC-based privilege escalation), (b) 11:48:14 — net user (local-account enumeration), (c) 11:48:31 — net group \"Domain Admins\" /domain (domain-account enumeration — checks whether RDP-GW-01 is domain-joined and which accounts are Domain Admins = next-target list). The 33-second compression of these three commands = automated or scripted (no human types 'whoami /priv' then 'net user' then 'net group Domain Admins /domain' in 33 seconds with consideration).", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: three Process Create events with parentImage=cmd.exe and image=(whoami.exe | net.exe), all under user=CORP\\administrator within 33 seconds of the 4624 success. Diagnostic of post-auth attacker orientation, not legitimate admin workflow.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:47:58+0200"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Lateral Movement (Attempted, Failed)", "technique": "T1021.001", "what_happened": "At 11:49:08, the attacker launched mstsc.exe /v:10.10.20.8 from explorer.exe on RDP-GW-01 — RDP from the compromised gateway to internal file server FILESVR-INT-02 (10.10.20.8). At 11:49:14 and 11:49:21, FILESVR-INT-02 logged 2 Event 4625 failures from ipAddress=10.10.5.10 (RDP-GW-01) targetUserName=administrator. The lateral RDP attempt FAILED — FILESVR-INT-02's local administrator password DIFFERS from RDP-GW-01's. Good local-admin password hygiene on FILESVR-INT-02 saved this host from compromise. The attacker stopped retrying after 2 failures — likely to avoid generating noisy lateral-failure alerts and switched tactics.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 on RDP-GW-01: image=mstsc.exe + commandLine='mstsc.exe /v:10.10.20.8' + parentImage=explorer.exe + user=CORP\\administrator. Windows Security Event 4625 on FILESVR-INT-02: ipAddress=10.10.5.10 + targetUserName=administrator + logonType=10. The two events linked by timestamp + source IP = same lateral attempt observed from both ends.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:49:08+0200"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Persistence / Defense Evasion (Registry Tamper)", "technique": "T1112 + T1547", "what_happened": "At 11:49:42, the attacker ran 'reg add \"HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\" /v fDenyTSConnections /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f' on RDP-GW-01. The registry value fDenyTSConnections=0 means 'do NOT deny Terminal Services (RDP) connections' = RDP enabled. The attacker is verifying/forcing RDP-enabled state so that even if a defender attempts to disable RDP on this gateway, the attacker's regkey override re-enables it on next service start. Defense-evasion + persistence touch combined.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=reg.exe + commandLine contains 'fDenyTSConnections' + 'REG_DWORD /d 0' + parentImage=cmd.exe + user=CORP\\administrator. The fDenyTSConnections=0 setting is a known RDP-persistence indicator (MITRE T1112 Modify Registry).", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T11:49:42+0200"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-09-22T11:34:48+0200", "end": "2026-09-22T11:49:42+0200"},
  "alert_count": 9,
  "unique_steps": 6
}
